                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_279\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix (fix_commit_diff.txt) changes the ESC-key handling condition in Minecraft.java from\n`if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){ pause(); } else { Mouse.setGrabbed(false); }`\nto\n`if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel || !isOnline()){ pause(); } else { Mouse.setGrabbed(false); }`.\n\nThis exact `if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){ ... } else { Mouse.setGrabbed(false); }` construct\nwas first introduced by candidate_279. Before that commit, the ESC key handler at that location\nsimply called `pause();` unconditionally. candidate_279 replaced it with the conditional logic\nthat routes ESC to `Mouse.setGrabbed(false)` instead of `pause()` whenever\n`packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` is true, without considering whether the player was actually\nonline/connected to a server. Since `isLoadingLevel` could be true (or not properly reset) in a\nsingleplayer context, pressing ESC would never call `pause()`, meaning the pause menu would fail\nto show up in singleplayer \u2014 exactly the bug described in the fix commit message (\"Fixed a bug\nwhich was causing the menu to not show up in singleplayer\").\n\nThe subsequent fix commit (matching candidate_281 in the candidate set, which is essentially\nidentical to fix_commit_diff.txt) patches this by adding `|| !isOnline()` to the condition, so\nthat in singleplayer (offline) the pause menu always shows regardless of the isLoadingLevel state.\nNo candidate between 279 and 281 alters this specific condition, confirming candidate_279 as the\ncommit that introduced the bug.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
